2026-08-16 22:30:12 [INFO   ] main: CONCLUSION: Gaussian Tc-broadening DOES narrow the worst-field |error| against Dan'kov et al.'s three points, from 48.9% (sharp model, sigma_Tc=0) to 20.9% at sigma_Tc=5.0K, driven mainly by the 1T point (+48.9% -> +20.9%). This comes with a real trade-off, not a clean win: the 5T error moves the OTHER direction, from -7.5% (sharp) to -14.2% (broadened) -- broadening lowers the 1-2T overestimate but deepens the 5T underestimate, consistent with a global smoothing of the transition rather than a field-selective fix. NOTE: sigma_Tc=5.0K is the LARGEST value swept here, and the 1T error is still falling monotonically with sigma at that edge ([48.9, 45.6, 42.4, 36.3, 30.7, 20.9] for sigma=[0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0] respectively) -- so this sweep has NOT located an interior optimum, only shown the direction of the trend within a physically plausible 0-5K range; a wider sweep (and, ideally, a real digitized dTad(T) curve to fit against, which this repo does not have) would be needed before treating any specific sigma_Tc as a calibrated value rather than a sensitivity finding.
